%S A399051 2,2,2,2,2,2,2,8,2,4,2,134,4,2,64,64,146,2,428,8,8,278,274,4,2,64,8,
%T A399051 16,4,64,16,394,64,16,634,16,2,128,764,16,128,466,8,586,478,478,1492,
%U A399051 4,8,8,794,542,8,1186,542,1618,1024,746,998,512,634,626,32,256
%N A399051 a(n) is the least even number c such that (prime(n)#/2) - c is a prime greater than prime(n).
%C A399051 A Fortunate-type function for the odd primorial. If c is even and c = 2^k*m with m odd, then any prime factor of m that is at most prime(n) divides both the primorial and c, hence divides the difference; so for a(n) < prime(n)^2 the odd part of a(n) is either 1 or a single prime exceeding prime(n). This holds for n=1..694, and conjecturally a(n) < prime(n)^2 always, in analogy with Fortune's conjecture.
%H A399051 Hossein Sadeghpour, <a href="/A399051/b399051.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 3..701</a>
%e A399051 a(11) = 4 because 3*5*7*...*31 = 100280245065 and that minus 4 is prime, while minus 2 is not.
%t A399051 primorial[n_] := Product[Prime[i], {i, n}]; Table[c = 2; While[! PrimeQ[primorial[n]/2 - c], c += 2]; c, {n, 3, 66}]
%o A399051 (PARI) my(M=3); forprime(p=5, 320, M*=p; my(c=2); while(!(M-c>p && ispseudoprime(M-c)), c+=2); print1(c, ", "))
